{
  "note": "Dedup lookup and commit transactions, docs/contracts/chunking-and-commit.md sections 5-6. lookup: what PUT/UploadPart does for a (domain, hash) given the consistent Chunks row (found=false: absent) and the state/class of its canonical pack generation; pin=rejected means R1 refused to protect that resource. reuse = reference the existing location after the pin, insert = write a blob then PutChunkIfAbsent, revive = write a Warm blob then CAS the location. transactions: exact DynamoDB TransactWriteItems actions per operation, conditions in the contract table. Fixture identities: bucket demo (bucket_id bucket-1, owner owner), grantee writer, key file, credential <caller>-key, reservation r-1, op_id op-1 (also the outbox event_id), writer lease lease-1, upload upload-1, pack pack-a generation gen-1, chunk default#SHA-256(hello). scenarios: one object key; state is the authoritative items the commit transaction touches (head of the key, owner counters, one reservation ledger per op with its r/s, upload state, outbox events as op -> committed head revision). Each op's writer follows section 6.4: commit reads the head consistent and sends a new parameter set (expected forces a stale expected revision with base 0), retry resends the last parameter set and token; lose_ack applies the transaction but the writer sees an unknown outcome; token_expired makes DynamoDB treat the token as new; set changes state outside the writer (limit, revoke credential/user/bucket/grant, fence_lost of an op, unavailable metadata). expect checks the outcome, the head revision returned, the number of TransactWriteItems the writer has sent, and optionally state; final is the whole state after the last step. tokens: ClientRequestToken for (op_id, attempt), null when invalid. refcount: worker batches of section 6.6; fill generates keys prefix000..prefix(count-1) with that refcount (state) or +1 each (event); every event's Outbox item starts with refcount_next = 0 (written by the commit); apply reads refcount_next consistent then runs batches and stops when the item is missing, crash_after stops right after that batch committed; resend sends that batch number without reading progress first (a late retry held by a worker); cleanup deletes the Outbox item only if refcount_next equals the number of batches, else it is refused. expect.progress lists refcount_next of the Outbox items that still exist.",
